934:
In the event of declaration of a Level One period of restricted water use, the following restrictions shall apply within the county:
(1) Outdoor water use may occur on scheduled days within the hours of 12:00 midnight to 10:00 a.m. and 4:00 p.m. to 12:00 midnight;
(2) Scheduled days for odd-numbered addresses are Tuesdays, Thursdays, and Sundays;
(3) Scheduled days for even-numbered addresses are Mondays, Wednesdays, and Saturdays;
(4) Use of hydrants for any purpose other than firefighting, public health, safety or flushing is prohibited.

935:
In the event of declaration of a Level Two period of restricted water use, the following restrictions shall apply within the county:
(1) Outdoor water use may occur on scheduled days within the hours of 12:00 midnight to 10:00 a.m.;
(2) Scheduled days for odd-numbered addresses are Tuesdays, Thursdays, and Sundays;
(3) Scheduled days for even-numbered addresses and golf course fairways are Mondays, Wednesdays, and Saturdays.
(4) The following uses are prohibited:
a. Using hydrants for any purpose other than firefighting, public health, safety, or flushing;
b. Washing hard surfaces such as streets, gutters, sidewalks, and driveways except when necessary for public health and safety.

936:
In the event of declaration of a Level Three period of restricted water use, the following restrictions shall apply within the county:
(1) Outdoor water use may occur on the scheduled day within the hours of 12:00 midnight to 10:00 a.m.;
(2) The scheduled day for odd-numbered addresses is Sunday;
(3) The scheduled day for even-numbered addresses and golf course fairways is Saturday;
(4) The following uses are prohibited:
a. Using hydrants for any purpose other than firefighting, public health, safety, or flushing;
b. Washing hard surfaces such as streets, gutters, sidewalks, and driveways except when necessary for public health and safety;
c. Filling installed swimming pools except when necessary for health care or structural integrity;
d. Washing vehicles such as cars, boats, trailers, motorbikes, airplanes, and golf carts;
e. Washing buildings or structures except for immediate fire protection;
f. Noncommercial fund-raisers, such as carwashes;
g. Using water for ornamental purposes such as fountains, reflecting pools, and waterfalls except when necessary to support aquatic life.

938:
The following uses are exempt from the use restrictions of this article unless the board of commissioners or county manager, at the time of declaration of a period of restricted water use, shall specifically designate one or more of the exemptions inapplicable, and provided that in exercising an exemption water use conservation practices should be implemented wherever possible:
(1) Capture and reuse of cooling system condensate or stormwater in compliance with applicable provisions of this Code and any technical code adopted by the county;
(2) Reuse of gray water in compliance with applicable provisions of this Code and any technical codes adopted by the county;
(3) Use of reclaimed wastewater for landscaping purposes by a designated user from a system permitted by EPD to provide reclaimed wastewater;
(4) Irrigation of personal food gardens;
(5) Except during Level Four restrictions, irrigation of landscapes that have been installed for less than 30 days and by persons who are not certified or licensed professionals, any day of the week, but only during allowed hours for the restriction level in effect, and then only for a period of 30 days following installation;
(6) For golf courses, use of reclaimed wastewater by a designated user from a system permitted by EPD to provide reclaimed wastewater, irrigation of fairways during times of nondrought and Level One restrictions, irrigation of tees during times of nondrought and Level One, Two, or Three restrictions, and irrigation of greens;
(7) Professionally certified or licensed landscapers, golf course contractors, and sports turf landscapers, but during installation and 30 days following installation only;
(8) Irrigation contractors, but during installation and as needed for proper maintenance and adjustments only;
(9) Sod producers, ornamental growers, fruit and vegetable growers, retail garden center, hydro-seeding, power washing, construction sites, producers of food and fiber, and carwashes;
(10) Water in of pesticides and herbicides on turf;
(11) Use of water for firefighting, health, sanitation, and medical purposes; and
(12) Other activities essential to daily business.

961:
Detached single-family developments as defined in this chapter must adhere to the following conditions:
(1) The developer/subdivider of any subdivision shall provide adequate streets and shall pave said streets according to minimum requirements as provided in this chapter; provided, that any proposed development which shall be entirely located along a public road, such that said public road serves as the sole road from which access to each lot is gained, shall prior to approval agree to and submit plans to make all improvements of such public road as is, in the opinion of the director of roads, the zoning administrator, the planning commission, and/or the board of commissioners, necessary to avoid and prevent hazard or danger to the public.
(2) On any development of three acres or more and comprised of more than four lots, a soil survey prepared by a soil scientist registered in the state shall be submitted with an application for rezoning or subdivision approval.
(3) A permit must be obtained from the county public works department for any street or driveway entering a county road. The permit for a driveway must be obtained before a building permit will be issued except where curb cut and gutter has been previously installed.
(4) A temporary construction exit must be installed and maintained while construction and/or grading equipment will be leaving a construction site to a public right-of-way, street, or parking area until construction is completed. The construction exit may be the same as the driveway exit.
a. The temporary construction exit pad shall be adequate to substantially eliminate the transport of mud from the construction site by either motor vehicles and equipment or from water run-off.
b. Any materials or mud spilled, dropped, washed, or tracked from vehicles or from the site onto roadways or into storm drains must be removed within 24 hours or the county may perform any necessary corrective work and the owner/developer shall reimburse the county for any expenses incurred. In the event the county undertakes the corrective work, neither building permits nor certificates of occupancy for said development shall be issued until said expenses have been reimbursed to the county.
c. The exit pad of gravel or stone must at least be 12 feet in width by 20 feet in length with a depth of four inches. The pad may be required to be an additional width, length, and/or depth if, in the opinion of the public works department, conditions so warrant.
d. In addition, all construction sites must comply with chapter 16, article VI of this Code.
(5) A developer may construct a single-family subdivision in which the streets contained therein are not dedicated to the county only upon compliance with all of the following:
a. All provisions of this chapter shall apply to the development of said subdivision except section 50-12(3)i, which provides for the streets to be dedicated to the county;
b. There shall be only one entrance/exit from the subdivision to a public street. In no event shall a private street be constructed so as to permit said street, in any configuration, to serve as a connecting street between two public streets. Other than the entrance/exit to the private subdivision, no private street shall adjoin any property outside the limits of the private subdivision;
c. The plat of the subdivision shall reflect and reserve all necessary utility easements and private rights-of-way to service the subdivision;
d. All plats shall be labeled as a "private subdivision" and shall contain on the face thereof the following language in prominent bold capital letters:
"THE STREETS IN THIS SUBDIVISION ARE PRIVATE STREETS AND ARE NEITHER MAINTAINED BY HARALSON COUNTY NOR CONSIDERED PART OF THE ROAD SYSTEM OF HARALSON COUNTY. THE RESPONSIBILITY FOR THE UPKEEP AND MAINTENANCE OF THE STREETS SHOWN HEREON ARE THE RESPONSIBILITY OF THE INDIVIDUAL HOMEOWNERS THROUGH THE SUBDIVISION'S INCORPORATED HOMEOWNER'S ASSOCIATION";
e. All deeds of conveyance for individual lots shall contain on the face thereof the following language in prominent bold capital letters:
"THE GRANTEE HEREIN ACKNOWLEDGES THAT ANY AND ALL MEANS OF INGRESS AND EGRESS TO THE PROPERTY CONVEYED HEREIN ALONG THE STREETS OF THE SUBDIVISION AS OUTLINED ON THE PLAT OF SAID SUBDIVISION ARE PRIVATE STREETS AND ARE NEITHER MAINTAINED BY HARALSON COUNTY NOR CONSIDERED PART OF THE ROAD SYSTEM OF HARALSON COUNTY. THE RESPONSIBILITY FOR THE UPKEEP AND MAINTENANCE OF THE STREETS SHOWN THEREON ARE THE RESPONSIBILITY OF THE INDIVIDUAL HOMEOWNERS THROUGH THE SUBDIVISION'S INCORPORATED HOMEOWNER'S ASSOCIATION AND NOT HARALSON COUNTY";
f. The developer shall provide proof to the zoning administrator from the state secretary of state of the incorporation of the subdivision's homeowners' association and proof that said association holds title to the right-of-way of the streets on behalf of the purchasers and requires all purchasers of lots in said subdivision to be members of said homeowners' association;
g. Place at the entrance to said subdivision a sign, approved by the zoning administrator, indicating that the streets in the subdivision are private streets;
h. If a gate or other controlled access is installed at the entrance to the subdivision, a place which provides emergency access to the subdivision by emergency agencies must first be approved by the emergency agencies of the county. Access must also be provided to anyone providing utilities to said subdivision; and
i. All agreements, restrictive covenants and other documentation related to the development shall be furnished to and approved by the zoning administrator, prior to commencement of development.
(Ord. of 12-7-2004, art. VI, § 6.7)

